<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <!--
      Always on, never gated on $(CI).

      This used to read Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'". Build-Release.ps1 does not set CI, so on a
      local release build the source paths were never remapped and the compiled binary embedded
      the full build path - including the developer's Windows username and their folder layout.
      A security audit found the shipped .exe carrying
      C:\Users\<name>\...\LocalPhotoPDF.Core.pdb, in both the ZIP and the installer.

      An unsigned public release that quietly names the machine it was built on is a privacy leak
      that no checksum or attestation catches, because both faithfully attest the leaking file.
    -->
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild>true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <!--
      ⛔ RuntimeFrameworkVersion DELIBERATELY DOES NOT LIVE HERE. It is set in
      src\LocalPhotoPDF\LocalPhotoPDF.csproj, which carries the full reasoning.

      It was put here first, on 2026-09-18, and it broke the build within the hour. A property in
      this file applies to EVERY project including LocalPhotoPDF.Tests, which is framework-
      dependent: pinning it made testhost.exe demand a runtime installed on the machine that no
      released SDK ships, and the entire suite aborted before a single test ran.

      The app bundles its runtime from NuGet at publish time; the test project takes its runtime
      from the machine at run time. Pinning is correct for the first and impossible for the second,
      so the pin belongs to the project, not the repository.
    -->
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>moderate</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
